/**
 * @defgroup interrupts FlightOS Interrupt Subsystem
 *
 * @brief Architecture-independent interrupt registration and dispatch with
 *        architecture-specific controller and trap support.
 *
 * @page interrupts_overview Interrupt Subsystem Overview
 *
 * The interrupt subsystem separates the kernel interrupt-service contract from
 * the processor and interrupt-controller mechanisms. Generic kernel and device
 * code describe interrupt ownership, handler execution priority, CPU affinity,
 * and controller priority through an abstract operation table. The selected
 * architecture implements that table and connects controller events to the
 * processor trap entry path.
 *
 * ## Concept and structure
 *
 * The high-level layer in `include/kernel/` and `kernel/` contains the handler
 * return convention, request metadata, and one registered controller backend.
 * Requests carry an interrupt number, immediate or deferred execution policy,
 * a handler, and opaque handler data. The generic layer delegates hardware
 * operations to the registered backend; support for multiple simultaneous
 * backend objects is not present.
 *
 * The current SPARC backend maintains separate primary interrupt (IRL) and
 * extended interrupt (EIRL) handler vectors. Handler registrations use fixed
 * pools, and deferred work uses a separate fixed queue pool. Per-interrupt CPU
 * affinity drives controller mask state. Optional interrupt statistics and rate
 * protection are controlled by configuration; rate protection is disabled in
 * the current source. Locking for all resources shared between CPUs is marked
 * as incomplete in the backend and needs review.
 *
 * ## Architectural design
 *
 * The architecture path begins when the SPARC trap table receives an
 * asynchronous interrupt. Interrupt levels 1 through 14 enter the common IRQ
 * trap entry; level 15 is handled separately as NMI in the trap table. The IRQ
 * entry saves the register state, configures the processor interrupt level
 * according to nested-IRQ configuration, and passes control to the SPARC
 * dispatcher. The dispatcher walks handlers for the selected primary or
 * extended interrupt, runs immediate handlers on the interrupt path, and queues
 * deferred handlers. The trap entry then restores the saved state and returns
 * to interrupted execution.
 *
 * On LEON3 and LEON4, primary interrupt numbers are represented by controller
 * bits 1 through 15 and extended interrupts occupy the 16 through 31 range.
 * LEON2 uses a different extended-interrupt numbering convention, translated
 * by the architecture macros. The exact controller behavior for every supported
 * build configuration needs review.
 *
 * Local interrupt state is represented by the SPARC PSR PIL field. The
 * architecture helpers save, disable, restore, or clear that field; generic
 * code reaches them through the asm-generic wrapper.
 *
 * ## Layer relationship and portability
 *
 * During boot, the architecture initializes its interrupt-controller
 * representation and publishes it to the generic kernel layer. The dependency
 * direction is deliberate: high-level kernel services and device drivers
 * depend on the generic contract, while that contract depends on the selected
 * architecture backend for target-specific behavior. Immediate and deferred
 * handler policies express the same separation in time: the architecture
 * recognizes and dispatches the event, while the kernel-facing contract
 * distinguishes work that may remain on the interrupt path from work that can
 * be completed later.
 *
 * This design allows the high-level interrupt model to remain usable when a
 * different processor or interrupt controller is selected. Porting the
 * subsystem consequently requires a new architecture-side realization of the
 * contract and its entry path, not changes to every kernel service that
 * consumes interrupts.
 *
 * ## High-level to architecture mapping
 *
 * | High-level concern | Generic kernel interface/core | SPARC implementation |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | Handler registration and backend contract | `include/kernel/irq.h`, `kernel/irq.c` | `arch/sparc/kernel/irq.c` |
 * | Local interrupt state | `include/asm-generic/irqflags.h` | `arch/sparc/include/asm/irqflags.h`, `arch/sparc/kernel/irq.c` |
 * | Controller access and IRQ numbering | no hardware-specific code | `arch/sparc/include/irq.h`, `arch/sparc/include/asm/irq.h`, `arch/sparc/kernel/irq.c` |
 * | Trap entry and return path | no hardware-specific code | `arch/sparc/kernel/irqtrap.S`, `arch/sparc/include/asm/ttable.h` |
 *
 * The existing @ref irqsys group contains detailed declarations, backend
 * documentation, and the original generic and architecture-specific diagrams.
 * The diagrams below provide the subsystem overview without enumerating API
 * calls.
 *
 * ## Diagrams
 *
 * @plantumlfile interrupts_architecture.puml
 *
 * @plantumlfile interrupts_flow.puml
 *
 * @plantumlfile interrupts_dispatch_activity.puml
 */
